WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:03.059
Some volcanoes we know well.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.380
They call attention to themselves
with towering ash plumes,

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.380
fiery flows of lava,

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.459
and the small earthquakes
that often precede eruptions.

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:15.059
Then there are volcanoes like Mt. Gede,

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:18.579
an active volcano that looms
over the western tip

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.460
of the Indonesian island of Java.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.280
Millions of people live on its flanks,
and in nearby cities.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.340
The volcano’s last outburst was in 1957,

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.260
long before modern monitoring 
equipment was put in place,

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.540
so scientists know little about
Gede’s temperament.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.140
How much warning will
people get before it erupts?

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:43.040
A new technique called diffusion 
chronometry is providing answers.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.580
It depends on the idea that tiny crystals,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:52.180
harvested from erupted ash and rock, 
contain stopwatches.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.820
The crystals first grow out of magma
in chambers deep underground.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.960
If something happens to the environment where
they are growing,

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.540
these crystals can grow rims
that are chemically different —

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.840
like rings inside a tree.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.100
For instance, when a new pulse of magma comes
into the chamber --

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.340
potentially triggering an eruption --

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.030
it often has a slightly different chemistry,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.640
which is recorded in the growth ring.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.770
The crystals continue to sit in the chamber

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.890
for months, years, or longer,

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.040
until the volcano finally erupts.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.540
During that waiting time,

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.900
the boundary between the
crystal core and rim gets blurred.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:37.040
Atoms diffuse across that boundary, making
each side less chemically distinct.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.800
Using a microprobe, scientists
can measure the shape

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.880
of that more gradual chemical transition

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.910
across the crystal face.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.550
The shape, in turn,

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:49.110
can be used to calculate the length of time

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.960
the crystals sat in the magma
chamber before erupting.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.880
At Mt. Gede, researchers found rocks
and ash from five different eruptions

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.560
that occurred thousands of years ago.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:04.290
By examining the chemical diffusion in crystals
from those rocks,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:08.190
they found that, in each eruption,
about a month elapsed

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.360
between the last pulse of magma
and the eruption itself

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.580
-- useful information for hazard planners.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:20.950
Of the more than 1,500 potentially active
volcanoes on Earth, only a small fraction

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:26.940
are actively monitored, and fewer still have
erupted since scientists started watching.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.430
With diffusion chronometry, researchers
can collect crystal in rocks in different

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:36.040
eruptions and use them to learn about the
personalities of these silent volcanoes.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.700
Thus helping them understand
a volcano’s future

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.840
by using its crystal clocks
to look deep into its past.

